938
00:31:01,660 --> 00:31:04,980
One thing to note about this instruction,

939
00:31:04,980 --> 00:31:06,440
is that inside it,

940
00:31:06,440 --> 00:31:08,920
it actually doesn't carry many parameters.

941
00:31:08,920 --> 00:31:10,440
without too many parameters

942
00:31:10,440 --> 00:31:11,500
So this one,

943
00:31:11,500 --> 00:31:12,640
but,

944
00:31:12,640 --> 00:31:14,840
when we talk about system calls,

945
00:31:14,840 --> 00:31:16,240
there's definitely not just one,

946
00:31:16,240 --> 00:31:16,720
right?

947
00:31:16,720 --> 00:31:17,540
With ecall,

948
00:31:17,540 --> 00:31:18,780
it just triggers the system call,

949
00:31:18,780 --> 00:31:20,460
but our actual system calls

950
00:31:20,460 --> 00:31:21,080
could be numerous,

951
00:31:21,080 --> 00:31:21,620
right?

952
00:31:21,620 --> 00:31:24,420
For example, look at Linux — there are many system call functions,

953
00:31:24,420 --> 00:31:25,700
open, read, write,

954
00:31:25,700 --> 00:31:26,640
